Hello,


Can you, please, send me the exact code you're using? (I.e. exactly the
>> value of CLASSPATH, your platform, how you started R and initialized rJava
>> and what you tried to do to load the class).
>>
>
Platform is RH Enterprise Linux 5 on Intel. Started R from commandline as
R.

==Code==
library(rJava)
.jinit();
cfg <- .jnew("org/apache/hadoop/conf/Configuration")
path1 <-
.jnew("org/apache/hadoop/fs/Path",paste(Sys.getenv("HADOOP_CONF_DIR"),"/hadoop-default.xml",sep=""))
path2 <-
.jnew("org/apache/hadoop/fs/Path",paste(Sys.getenv("HADOOP_CONF_DIR"),"/hadoop-site.xml",sep=""))
.jcall(cfg,"V","addResource", path1)
.jcall(cfg,"V","addResource", path2)
.jcall("org/apache/hadoop/fs/FileSystem","Lorg/apache/hadoop/fs/FileSystem;","get",cfg)
filesystem = FileSystem.get(config);
##filesystem is NULL
.jgetEx()
[1] "Java-Object{java.lang.RuntimeException:
java.lang.ClassNotFoundException:
org.apache.hadoop.hdfs.DistributedFileSystem}"

All the above classes, including the one not found are in
hadoop-0.19.2-dev-core.jar.
Strangley, cfg,path1,path2 are not null.  I also tried passing a  vector of
the jar files to classpath jar files to .jinit but still got the same
result.
Yet when I create the JVM (as I described), then I I dont get the class not
found error.

Quickly looking through the hadoop sources, it does do some sort of
reflection  - does that have any bearing?

Thank you
Saptarshi

